#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275344 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064321 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "639c3818-9c51-5dc5-abd5-c833fec45ac1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09d8a938-bb8b-586d-a009-80b0510ce9f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272474} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd21c7b-2efc-5787-a0bf-3fa6605305c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273059} {:answer/id #uuid "5078832f-99e5-5a1e-a221-d1c25279772b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273068} {:answer/id #uuid "9f72c57d-bf7b-549c-b833-959350356303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273764} {:answer/id #uuid "a8a00c31-a5b6-502d-b5ac-115de2782659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8a00c31-a5b6-502d-b5ac-115de2782659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273845} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c385e53-9b12-55c7-9a12-b7e039870860" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46d114bb-3749-5f15-a0c8-60e33e9a25dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-156193" :question/problem "![](images/36/3676c09e0fa21bbf0a0d1828fbb6e6095f83965ea3edd0538786ca11d2fb317e.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(2).$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=2,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](images/6d/6dad371f36d3d5cbb88bbed40b2e3b60f94fbcad8284b0e218093c13c57d6e41.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-2,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [0,2],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a triangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\underbrace{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{1}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-2,0],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2+3) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [0,2],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=5+2=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064321 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "639c3818-9c51-5dc5-abd5-c833fec45ac1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d987504-13b8-5ad4-93f5-30f05ca460e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272652} {:answer/id #uuid "63ad3ffc-b7b8-5588-b9a4-d9bf077b2faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273244} {:answer/id #uuid "67452acd-e391-5e02-9acc-1c451b0ea8ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273274} {:answer/id #uuid "6e000460-8cff-5300-81a7-8948948bcf2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273343} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c6f6f6-3f45-5821-9ddd-68474d19acd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1c6f6f6-3f45-5821-9ddd-68474d19acd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273923} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaf0d01c-1750-583d-a717-14c22e8db8e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f35a3fe-7bad-544a-83dd-b6cbe771137f" :question/math-academy-id "q-156194" :question/problem "![](images/1a/1a3b8289936cd2597ac1252e2b28987f245a7c4e88dcf3677e1ba3626460688b.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](images/b5/b5becb438f3bc8ab5445e116ddb64bf168013301838231bdc4d32d34283da5f1.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [0,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [1,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is another trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\underbrace{{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{1}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [0,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (2+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [1,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the triapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2+3) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}+5=\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133808 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e33c97-0499-5829-9886-be30b00989ae" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Multiple Linear Regression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11b911ee-b194-542e-bba1-bc4776b17f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 11 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 15 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275635} {:answer/id #uuid "6db58e86-941e-5f56-8b4a-029179442a54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 6 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 11 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 15 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276456} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad7ee8e-c946-52f1-aa32-b09564ba5d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498