{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290350 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073714 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58e2e40e-209b-5bc7-98df-11841c2bbcc3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Midpoint of a Segment With Variable Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62d860d4-9969-5b3f-a5f8-05df5bab25cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4p" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62d860d4-9969-5b3f-a5f8-05df5bab25cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4p" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288723} :answer-field/id #uuid "54505da0-7d85-511d-9082-6005dc121606" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289910} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8907f96d-60b6-565b-9452-eefecd8a5cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9q}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8907f96d-60b6-565b-9452-eefecd8a5cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9q}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288955} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9a598c6-6c8b-5d41-b174-97a44daeeddc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3ec4ca5d-eff6-5bd3-b9ef-6440c83a0ef0" :question/math-academy-id "q-216937" :question/problem "The midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(4p,2q)$ and $(-12p,7q)$\n is$\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $({x}_{m},{y}_{m})$ be the midpoint between $\\underbrace{(4p,2q)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and $\\underbrace{(-12p,7q)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})}.$\n\nWe use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{m},{y}_{m}) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4p+(-12p)}{2},\\frac{2q+7q}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-8p}{2},\\frac{9q}{2}) \\\\  & =(-4p,\\frac{9q}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073813 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3ee7d83-2897-53ea-aa8f-05d52cb1f9e7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Points in Three Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bd86069-1b4a-5b24-b9f7-2433cb83b2af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bd86069-1b4a-5b24-b9f7-2433cb83b2af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291228} :answer-field/id #uuid "aff9074e-63a9-5156-8b4a-cba9e66096fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea8b80f5-5852-58eb-a9f5-87a9a29aa672" :question/math-academy-id "q-262213" :question/problem "The distance between the points $A(-1,4,-1)$ and $B(2,6,5)$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}{)}^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1}{)}^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1}{)}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A\\underbrace{(-1,4,-1)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B\\underbrace{(2,6,5)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(2-(-1){)}^{2}+(6-4{)}^{2}+(5-(-1){)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292613 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073813 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3ee7d83-2897-53ea-aa8f-05d52cb1f9e7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Points in Three Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f915bfe-4a2c-54ce-8b1c-681017d22c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f915bfe-4a2c-54ce-8b1c-681017d22c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291326} :answer-field/id #uuid "484c534d-c8af-5196-86a2-ebd86bbf6ec8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c45e8a35-554d-5362-9dc0-c76aa841bc0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-262216" :question/problem "The distance between the points $A(5,-2,-3)$ and $B(2,2,-3)$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}{)}^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1}{)}^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1}{)}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A\\underbrace{(5,-2,-3)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B\\underbrace{(2,2,-3)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(2-5{)}^{2}+(2-(-2){)}^{2}+(-3-(-3){)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3{)}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073813 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3ee7d83-2897-53ea-aa8f-05d52cb1f9e7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Points in Three Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38a51c24-9e48-5dd8-a285-6a6c64b8aaac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38a51c24-9e48-5dd8-a285-6a6c64b8aaac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290865} :answer-field/id #uuid "3245847b-5278-5299-8f8d-bfaabbe9342c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e16c5fb0-60f5-53f8-bf40-4b5cf72b5d57" :question/math-academy-id "q-262221" :question/problem "The distance between the points $A(3,1,2)$ and $B(5,-3,4)$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A\\underbrace{(3,1,2)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B\\underbrace{(5,-3,4)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(5-3)^{2}+(-3-1)^{2}+(4-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073813 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3ee7d83-2897-53ea-aa8f-05d52cb1f9e7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Points in Three Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b117b5c6-f1c2-5ef7-a45b-f5d8047463c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b117b5c6-f1c2-5ef7-a45b-f5d8047463c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291403} :answer-field/id #uuid "74c64785-f0ae-58c8-8778-11cce9425723" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36c96529-7715-55dd-9853-b5f7e1d65f9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-262223" :question/problem "The distance between the points $A(4,-2,-2)$ and $B(3,-4,2)$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}{)}^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1}{)}^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1}{)}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A\\underbrace{(4,-2,-2)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B\\underbrace{(3,-4,2)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(3-4{)}^{2}+(-4-(-2){)}^{2}+(2-(-2){)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1{)}^{2}+(-2{)}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd03f31e-360e-5cc1-9118-f71f813d572e" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial With Only Two Real Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8727f0f6-ab35-52da-a633-a22f01cc6e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-3x+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8727f0f6-ab35-52da-a633-a22f01cc6e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-3x+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291209} :answer-field/id #uuid "817f7429-6163-58c5-814c-211579740270" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fd39389-dd69-52d2-8d61-181abc3b2657" :question/math-academy-id "q-270972" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that $2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-34{x}^{2}+56x-192=(x+4)(x-6)P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-34{x}^{2}+56x-192.$ We know that $(x+4)$ and $(x-6)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x+4)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+4)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $2$ | $-7$ | $-34$ | $56$ | $-192$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-8$ | $60$ | $-104$ | $192$ |\n|  | $2$ | $-15$ | $26$ | $-48$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-34{x}^{2}+56x-192}{x+4}=2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+26x-48,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-34{x}^{2}+56x-192=(x+4)(2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+26x-48).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-6)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+26x-48).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-6)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $6$ | $2$ | $-15$ | $26$ | $-48$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $12$ | $-18$ | $48$ |\n|  | $2$ | $-3$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+26x-48}{x-6}=2{x}^{2}-3x+8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-34{x}^{2}+56x-192 & =(x+4)(x-6)(2{x}^{2}-3x+8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial $2{x}^{2}-3x+8$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:\n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac=(-3)^{2}-4(2)(8)=-55<0\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial $2{x}^{2}-3x+8$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative. \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\n2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-34{x}^{2}+56x-192=(x+4)(x-6)\\underbrace{(2{x}^{2}-3x+8)}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $P(x)=2{x}^{2}-3x+8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078598 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9953dc9-2c7d-5852-ad69-5e764720a070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9953dc9-2c7d-5852-ad69-5e764720a070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296241} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee27c5da-d51f-54b3-b216-3b6dbb4ee380" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6302582-812c-5f14-9dac-2aabed1d56fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-316122" :question/problem "![](images/64/64ce1ae5f198ce41e55414d853b1bd99e73d747cddb80412b30246bfdb13a907.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,1)$ and the line segment for $x\\in (1,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,= & {\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,= & \\,\\underbrace{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{1}}\\,+\\,\\underbrace{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,1).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (1,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\, & =\\,{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\,8+6 \\\\  & =\\,14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078598 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b89600ee-2da7-5468-ad6a-bf2909f0e8eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b89600ee-2da7-5468-ad6a-bf2909f0e8eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296233} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae4e0025-0a6f-572e-8335-324e6ee2568a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbb60ffa-14d6-5751-913f-1131a36b5e4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-316132" :question/problem "![](images/b8/b871a949beaac0319d77364c43aa261bda8c28642f8bfc80a80f974fd7323cf3.png)\n\nThe graph $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,-1],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-1,1),$ and the line segment for $x\\in [1,3].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\underbrace{{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{1}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{-{A}_{2}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,-1].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,1).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,3].$ The corresponding region is a square, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-2+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064320 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a8be4bb-bbfe-5a5d-872c-91b4d67edc2f" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Accumulation Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caef1662-b802-571e-94e7-2100591edec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180+0.5{t}^{2}+3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "caef1662-b802-571e-94e7-2100591edec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180+0.5{t}^{2}+3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296330} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5a317d8-cf34-5d96-bff6-63043ecb3677" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d8f85d18-c70d-5c3e-acb8-1dd6b68eae84" :question/math-academy-id "q-316254" :question/problem "In $2005$, a group of scientists started tracking the population of sea turtles in a coastal sanctuary. The initial population was $180,$ and the birth and death rates are given by $4+1.5t$ and $1+0.5t,$ respectively, where $t$ is the number of years since $2005.$  \n\nFind an expression for the population $P(t)$ of sea turtles in the sanctuary at any time $t>0.$\n\n$P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $p(t)$ be the rate of change of the population. According to the given information,\n\n$$\np(t)=\\underbrace{4+1.5t}_{\\text{population increase}}-\\underbrace{(1+0.5t)}_{\\text{population decrease}}=t+3.\n$$\n\nLet $P(T)$ represent the population of turtles $T$ years after $2005.$ By the accumulation of change formula, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)=P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =180+{\\int }_{0}^{T}(t+3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =180+(\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}+3t){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =180+\\frac{1}{2}{T}^{2}+3T.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable  $t.$ We get \n\n$$\nP(t)=180+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}+3t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078599 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb30dbbf-4f83-59d0-b712-f0e592aafb27" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Curved Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29b5c61b-9655-552f-ac09-7fc05f32384f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29b5c61b-9655-552f-ac09-7fc05f32384f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295505} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9d42887-bd7b-5093-9106-9460fec8d94a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53c80807-8464-558f-b4e9-61601f18a79a" :question/math-academy-id "q-316261" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d36f2859865da58d226a738d8338f726df950db75b7421b604bb692c9049d54a.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,-2]$ and the line segment for $x\\in (-2,1].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\underbrace{{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{1}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-2].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-2,1].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi +9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd9167b4-572f-589f-9b94-b62ba57eb28f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4f22f82-3f4c-56a7-8fca-b1699296b03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi -4}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4f22f82-3f4c-56a7-8fca-b1699296b03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi -4}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296486} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5ebfc2e-32f9-5714-a71a-21c80776c9ec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e5a2c9b-15eb-580e-9b48-bc6a0c515d66" :question/math-academy-id "q-316309" :question/problem "![](images/1c/1c69b5a43e445861a728fcd8faed9e89b3bcb61141ad35d99e60f2a43983244d.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x),$ which is defined on $[-2,9],$ is shown above. It consists of three line segments and the arc of the semi-circle centered at $(5,0)$ with radius $3.$ \n\nFind the average value ${f}_{\\text{avg}}$ of $f(x)$ on $[0,8].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${f}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The average value of a function on a given interval $[a,b]$ is \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the additive property of the definite integral, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\underbrace{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{-{A}_{1}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{2}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe 1st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,2].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-2.\n$$\n\nThe 2nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [2,8].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{8}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula for the average value, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8-0}\\cdot (-2+\\frac{9\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{9\\pi }{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4+9\\pi }{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi -4}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299672 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100850 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f41b2bd-0c1d-5267-b91d-7aba35ea7efd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff8083fc-1c95-550c-ba02-c4f9d8d8cea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff8083fc-1c95-550c-ba02-c4f9d8d8cea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298644} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1adf289-d217-5847-bd5d-b2d7420e8453" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f931cf9f-2dac-54ec-8d65-3efbaae6ccc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-318223" :question/problem "![](images/55/551c38c3bedd56d8fc1c4fef9f1fcc15b9fbf6fa83af81253b7a4d2296aaeb38.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of five line segments. Find the value of \n${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-1]$, the line segment for $x\\in [-1,1]$, the three line segments for $x\\in [1,6].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\underbrace{{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{1}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{2}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{1}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{3}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-1].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)\\cdot h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,1].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,6].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)\\cdot h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(5+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =6+3+7 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100851 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75f1ac34-ae96-53af-b8ae-d465af28f75d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Nonlinear Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67fff17b-e32b-5109-899d-e0a14de50aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67fff17b-e32b-5109-899d-e0a14de50aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297999} :answer-field/id #uuid "a18e3e97-a4e0-5e84-b042-7a032d5dcbfb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c349db8f-5b7a-5939-87f5-078e4758e4b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-318225" :question/problem "![](images/95/95e601203c55e93922554e4eb56a9742f4a84466ac387e3358484baaf9287e08.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of a line segment and an arc of a semi-circle centered at $(4,0)$ with radius $2.$ Find the value of \n${\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,2]$ and the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in [2,6].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\underbrace{{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{1}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,2].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [2,6].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =2+2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119796 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e460dcd-59cf-54d2-80e6-c0d72b6bafdd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using Two or More Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0432df21-0cbf-5310-b63d-d6d35b252ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299948} {:answer/id #uuid "0e624142-c08f-57f0-ac81-3c8acbe9f224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300009} {:answer/id #uuid "934f2563-3041-51a5-be22-78f3bc46ae92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300701} {:answer/id #uuid "ac425112-4037-539d-b950-dc90094cf5b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300823} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c403d6-3218-5d7c-bb7b-d08ac835de9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e624142-c08f-57f0-ac81-3c8acbe9f224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300009} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0579342-0bba-5c5a-a576-6b032097c4cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "912ad134-e874-55d5-a192-6e414d5ad134" :question/math-academy-id "q-32533" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-0.5){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=0.5,{u}_{33}=1,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=0.5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44acbd5f-b359-597c-af07-f4df8077e41b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300303} {:answer/id #uuid "5fbb74af-0781-5c0d-a3b0-d192d46a427a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300451} {:answer/id #uuid "c3c014cd-34e6-5174-8a6c-ca3b5fd6d05c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300941} {:answer/id #uuid "d732659e-73b0-5b52-a39b-ff82b321e875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301032} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd631f2-7bb7-575a-aaaa-d8c9a58a508e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d732659e-73b0-5b52-a39b-ff82b321e875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301032} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b767a95-e4b2-5302-bbb4-fdf21380f70a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "883a308b-5dbc-5683-ad84-c19d4f942e32" :question/math-academy-id "q-32548" :question/problem "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=3,{u}_{22}=1,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119799 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7634b3ac-f9e6-514e-87ba-e78590c9fa53" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 Linear System Using LU Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3be1af41-0c7e-535a-9a86-a5c82122dcdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300250} {:answer/id #uuid "89b9efd6-ce79-56d0-af07-0e42c41ffec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300659} {:answer/id #uuid "98709d57-b5d1-505c-a13b-2435f487b4d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300728} {:answer/id #uuid "b5fc1198-c5b8-53d4-8b4a-c8b8adf38ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300866} {:answer/id #uuid "b65d5146-dbe1-5221-b3af-8171d063039f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98709d57-b5d1-505c-a13b-2435f487b4d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300728} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ff87f9e-a211-5118-b7af-25e270840e47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74c73bef-8f9e-5d05-b33f-448bad8fb053" :question/math-academy-id "q-32570" :question/problem "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{U},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of ${y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=b=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=-4 \\\\ 2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-6.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=-4$ and ${y}_{2}=2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=y=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2} &=-4 \\\\ -3{x}_{2} &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and ${x}_{1}=2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=-4+3(-\\frac{2}{3})=-6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4d10f94-98d4-53d8-a35c-e65a06dd2c55" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using a Single Elementary Row Operation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4710777-4113-5e2f-942d-646f4e53a9cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300860} {:answer/id #uuid "b49d7ad9-3cc8-57cb-be15-19370075ff70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300861} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b054fd-c776-5b51-8723-7b7aaccfda90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300961} {:answer/id #uuid "f9cabe7f-0c93-5428-b6ba-a289c1bad4c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301195} {:answer/id #uuid "fe295108-2b0f-575e-9cfd-8654adb62aa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9cabe7f-0c93-5428-b6ba-a289c1bad4c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301195} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3e791e8-fb69-5daf-a127-f8937694643d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8e3eee9-0aa0-52fc-bec7-7606d20a727c" :question/math-academy-id "q-32581" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 12 & 16 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 12 & 16 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6093e24d-14bb-5e3b-a0b4-7a562a12f6a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a Singular 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "313f7f73-ae71-5493-992f-4e48bea91c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300201} {:answer/id #uuid "31938138-7d2c-5bed-930a-21cc179190f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300205} {:answer/id #uuid "b073af7e-07ec-5fcd-bbd1-bcce7e503f63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300842} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d443bc-9fc8-52e6-ab7c-4ece0d4276c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300937} {:answer/id #uuid "eea969a6-a2e5-502c-8add-7b9d6854171a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1d443bc-9fc8-52e6-ab7c-4ece0d4276c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300937} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb7be8c1-9dd6-5e40-801c-bb8c984dfe84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54aa3fc3-c0fb-5a79-b3e0-2e2c9dd3e2c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-32597" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to reduce the matrix to the row echelon form. For every elementary row operation we also write the equivalent notation in terms of multiplication by the corresponding elementary matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-2,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d223293-daf7-5ae0-8b58-c8aedf7d5f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300364} {:answer/id #uuid "5f7b83a9-fc50-5523-9213-c5c55335f7c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300448} {:answer/id #uuid "6be9bb45-cb39-515d-9e76-50406807757d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300506} {:answer/id #uuid "8c9a021e-530d-5a1b-bbbc-f93201721114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300671} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0b30d6-e077-5ae5-9c6c-d0df1cb8c001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f0b30d6-e077-5ae5-9c6c-d0df1cb8c001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300765} :answer-field/id #uuid "107612e4-db9b-5cdc-9b16-c8ff3e069e6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85e17ab0-6cd0-5554-bbc1-74b553196159" :question/math-academy-id "q-32598" :question/problem "Given that\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -1 \\\\ -9 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -1 \\\\ -9 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-1,{u}_{22}=4,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{22}=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9279d979-74ba-5504-b98a-63692db3af72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4374f2ca-d187-5c74-a8e5-694af7f1a10f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300294} {:answer/id #uuid "4a359ac0-f626-5fc9-b0c1-01e620a91911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300341} {:answer/id #uuid "7a50d87f-d58a-5c34-a7a5-6614e8291acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300564} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a1b26d-8ffa-5c97-9580-8122a3e8e4fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300940} {:answer/id #uuid "d92efac6-01ae-5b2e-8354-9a9f798d247d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a359ac0-f626-5fc9-b0c1-01e620a91911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300341} :answer-field/id #uuid "62bd16e6-f4e0-5d3c-b02e-ace76daef0c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "468d9e20-789c-5294-9d35-e95d404e4f0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-32617" :question/problem "Given that \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{bmatrix}$ \nhas the LU decomposition $B=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $B$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $B$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{7}{3}){R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot B \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}B=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}B & =U \\\\ B & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ B & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{3} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=\\frac{7}{3},{u}_{12}=9,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=21.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5250e0e1-4909-5075-8a8e-aa2f1be0bd9a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution of an Intermediate System Using the LU Factorization of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31a584a4-1cb3-5a39-965f-bd37797a14dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300207} {:answer/id #uuid "5667e7ae-d6bd-5c1f-ad1e-aae452fb72f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300413} {:answer/id #uuid "8b7176ae-1c26-5712-bc49-4af26749cfe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300668} {:answer/id #uuid "eba296dc-899c-5301-ae53-afe3e0bf711e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301129} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f7fdf2-1c9e-50a6-8a62-a3a05cd4b6ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eba296dc-899c-5301-ae53-afe3e0bf711e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301129} :answer-field/id #uuid "c89816ba-4cb7-5469-8836-98801e44c13c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e67ea89-60e8-5377-8f1d-5084c5f474ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-32647" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $b,$ given by \n\n$$\nL=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $y$ such that \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $Ax=b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=2 \\\\ -4{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=2$ and ${y}_{2}=6.$ \n\nTherefore, $y=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119800 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fa9fbb8-b2bc-5c93-bb07-bc8c7ef7c115" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution of an Intermediate System Using the LU Factorization of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "227f8924-c424-5ace-a60e-ec0f75170bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 27\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300127} {:answer/id #uuid "92f848fa-5e70-5e9d-b0b2-39d1279bf9a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -27\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300699} {:answer/id #uuid "b6dd3cfd-0739-55c3-a7db-96724115278a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -27\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300873} {:answer/id #uuid "c66ecfa0-d3ff-5035-878c-474ec658c5bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300953} {:answer/id #uuid "ea19420c-93c3-5e7a-bd37-4cf627ed744e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92f848fa-5e70-5e9d-b0b2-39d1279bf9a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -27\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300699} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e3c053a-931d-5e1f-b30f-402efc4920cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6904caba-1f35-5875-862d-7c3f71474ab1" :question/math-academy-id "q-32671" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $b,$ given by \n\n$$\nL=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $3\\times 3$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $y$ such that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $Ax=b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=3 \\\\ -4{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =3, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-1+4{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-1+4(3) \\\\  & =11, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =4-3{y}_{1}-2{y}_{2} \\\\  & =4-3(3)-2(11) \\\\  & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -27\\end{bmatrix}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302309 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c91fb2b-5607-555d-b897-f0e76765dfa6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lower Triangular Matrix Given the Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f2ebc4b-5389-54bf-a2fa-d9bfc4a8f26e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300018} {:answer/id #uuid "2c9153f4-2e30-5c37-8b1e-09ae03d171c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300179} {:answer/id #uuid "432fa160-28fe-59c0-b329-f317a7313416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300293} {:answer/id #uuid "4acc2614-a841-5f02-8de0-c500389e0674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300346} {:answer/id #uuid "7907818c-57cc-5eaf-9979-1133fc70f621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4acc2614-a841-5f02-8de0-c500389e0674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300346} :answer-field/id #uuid "174c33e3-c3b7-56d0-872e-43efb4f0dfa2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "142d0252-9564-5605-b794-3e443fd3c338" :question/math-academy-id "q-32685" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\n$$\n\nGiven that $C$ has the LU factorization $C=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ C & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119796 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e460dcd-59cf-54d2-80e6-c0d72b6bafdd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using Two or More Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47fe83a0-e9d5-50a3-9940-fe6aa5b2fca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300325} {:answer/id #uuid "86d51352-51ec-57c4-9bf2-275890748404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300638} {:answer/id #uuid "c5cdd5e8-4939-5ef4-9d9c-85d5d69186c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300947} {:answer/id #uuid "ce4ba015-2615-5951-9a18-719c2ec77b4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300991} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ed7e67-5b4b-58b7-81f7-0658fc92406c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8ed7e67-5b4b-58b7-81f7-0658fc92406c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301190} :answer-field/id #uuid "b775ffec-3e99-500c-b43f-7235402bf13f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07c6f264-146a-54ea-b4f6-d999ec6ba735" :question/math-academy-id "q-32720" :question/problem "Given that\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $B=LU,$ then ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $B$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $B$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot B \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-0.25){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.25 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot B \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}B=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}B & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}B & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ B & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -0.25 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ B & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.25 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ B & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.25 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=0.25,{u}_{33}=-3,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=-0.75.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119796 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e460dcd-59cf-54d2-80e6-c0d72b6bafdd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using Two or More Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08da2c73-b3f3-5f9e-bdca-1b5b280e8ca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299976} {:answer/id #uuid "19ab99ee-abb1-5365-b563-f8e7f4d626a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300075} {:answer/id #uuid "19c8433d-ce31-55b1-ace0-59e14900aabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300076} {:answer/id #uuid "7190b1cb-7787-5bdb-9600-71dbc81662e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300527} {:answer/id #uuid "c9f2b7f6-5083-5e02-a198-e7a6dbb7f6c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19ab99ee-abb1-5365-b563-f8e7f4d626a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300075} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a4a2fa1-bb43-517d-8181-7f72736b0a79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1cd1efa-f609-59d5-a15c-c2a561c4fae4" :question/math-academy-id "q-32722" :question/problem "Given that\n$K=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $K=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $K$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $K$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{7}{4}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot K \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot K \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}K=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}K & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}K & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ K & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ K & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ K & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ K & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd03f31e-360e-5cc1-9118-f71f813d572e" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Quartic Polynomial With Only Two Real Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0382a5ea-db99-589b-81d2-d5c436068601" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}-x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0382a5ea-db99-589b-81d2-d5c436068601" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}-x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302478} :answer-field/id #uuid "a733200a-8113-59aa-ab5f-2b6d94152d94" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0b6781c6-fbc9-5b3d-86e2-c16bb96fea3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-328607" :question/problem "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that $-3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}-7x+60=-(x+3)(x-4)P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=-3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}-7x+60.$ We know that $(x+3)$ and $(x-4)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.  \n\nBecause $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division:  \n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $-3$ | $4$ | $30$ | $-7$ | $60$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $9$ | $-39$ | $27$ | $-60$ |\n|  | $-3$ | $13$ | $-9$ | $20$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,  \n\n$$\n\\frac{-3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}-7x+60}{x+3}=-3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}-9x+20,\n$$\n\nwhich means that  \n\n$$\n-3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}-7x+60=(x+3)(-3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}-9x+20).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-4)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(-3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}-9x+20).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division once more:  \n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $-3$ | $13$ | $-9$ | $20$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-12$ | $4$ | $-20$ |\n|  | $-3$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,  \n\n$$\n\\frac{-3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}-9x+20}{x-4}=-3{x}^{2}+x-5,\n$$\n\nwhich means that  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}-7x+60 & =(x+3)(x-4)(-3{x}^{2}+x-5) \\\\  & =-(x+3)(x-4)(3{x}^{2}-x+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial $3{x}^{2}-x+5$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:  \n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac=(-1)^{2}-4(3)(5)=-59<0.\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial $3{x}^{2}-x+5$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative.  \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by  \n\n$$\n-3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}-7x+60=-(x+3)(x-4)\\underbrace{(3{x}^{2}-x+5)}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $P(x)=3{x}^{2}-x+5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a47-840f-c823c9f70e36" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of First-Order Linear Systems of ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04215ae4-e362-5179-aa16-f64611cab85b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465981} {:answer/id #uuid "8a73ae01-e668-533a-8ee7-c6b89930becc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a73ae01-e668-533a-8ee7-c6b89930becc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465982} :answer-field/id #uuid "128db81a-f678-5e76-9297-f5984c2d1de0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466834} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7817a577-c492-5b10-ad8a-ada69e8ae0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465983} {:answer/id #uuid "5babd41a-b59c-5662-9f2a-5f8f64060536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7817a577-c492-5b10-ad8a-ada69e8ae0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465983} :answer-field/id #uuid "89355e5f-a26a-594a-b62a-a9482525ec6a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466835} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d5bc8dc-1011-53d6-be83-2431da52de28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465985} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7d5874-299d-503e-a6ec-50df4a134464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d5bc8dc-1011-53d6-be83-2431da52de28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465985} :answer-field/id #uuid "e260212c-56c8-5314-9942-156986da6d6c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8f297359-1573-550c-acfb-39ada6af0bec" :question/math-academy-id "q-330297" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n- **I.** $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- **II.** $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$\n- **III.** $3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+1\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $-1$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $3$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166901 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f6920b1-2a94-5a4